* Plumb in a bin dir
* Change the visual editor to be emacsclient ala https://wiki.archlinux.org/index.php/Emacs#As_a_daemon
* tmux-peer-latency reads SSH_CONNECTION from the tmux SESSION env, but the peer
  is a property of a CLIENT. Attaching the same session locally (no
  SSH_CONNECTION) makes it fall back to the global env and report latency to the
  last remote machine instead of nothing; two remote clients at once is
  ambiguous by construction. Moot while every attach comes from one machine.
  Fix: resolve per-client -- #{client_pid}, walk up to the sshd-session or
  mosh-server parent, read SSH_CONNECTION from its /proc/<pid>/environ. Linux
  only, so the Mac keeps the env-based path. NOT a mosh problem: mosh-server
  inherits SSH_CONNECTION from its ssh bootstrap and passes it on, and Tailscale
  IPs are stable per node, so roaming keeps the value correct.
